          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $K:S^1\\hookrightarrow S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot in the standard oriented three-sphere, and let $F\\subset S^3$ be a connected compact smooth oriented embedded surface with boundary $K$, oriented so that its boundary orientation is the given orientation of $K$. Let $g$ be the nonnegative integer determined by $\\chi(F)=1-2g$. Choose an ordered integral basis $a_1,\\ldots,a_{2g}$ of the first singular homology group $H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)$, represented by integral one-cycles on $F$. Orient the normal line of $F$ by requiring $(n,u,v)$ to be positively oriented in $S^3$ whenever $(u,v)$ is a positive tangent basis of $F$. The positive push-off $a_i^+$ is the cycle carried to a sufficiently small positive normal level in a bicollar of $F$.\n\nFor disjoint oriented closed curves $J,L\\subset S^3$, orient a meridian $\\mu_L$ of $L$ so that a positive tangent to $L$ followed by a positive basis of its normal disk gives the ambient orientation. The linking number $\\operatorname{lk}(J,L)$ is the integer determined by $[J]=\\operatorname{lk}(J,L)[\\mu_L]$ in $H_1(S^3\\setminus L;\\mathbb Z)$, using the Alexander-duality identification that sends $[\\mu_L]$ to $1$. Extend linking additively over integral one-cycles. The Seifert pairing is $\\theta_F(x,y)=\\operatorname{lk}(x^+,y)$, and its matrix in the chosen basis is $V=(V_{ij})_{i,j=1}^{2g}$, where $V_{ij}=\\theta_F(a_i,a_j)=\\operatorname{lk}(a_i^+,a_j)$. The surface and basis are part of the data; this definition asserts no knot-only matrix invariant.",
